{
  "summary": "Re-verify S1/S2/S3 IPNS signature-storage security findings against current code",
  "agentCount": 3,
  "logs": [],
  "result": [
    {
      "finding": "S1",
      "status": "partially-addressed",
      "summary": "S1 is still essentially OPEN, with a partial mitigation added since the todo. On publish, the server still base64-decodes dto.record (line 58), verifies only the Ed25519 signature against the IPNS name (lines 86-88), and stores latestCid = dto.metadataCid verbatim (lines 256, 303) — the embedded CID inside the signed record is NEVER parsed and compared against dto.metadataCid, and dto.expectedSequenceNumber is compared only against the DB column existing.sequenceNumber (lines 237-248), never against the embedded sequence. The one new addition is an anti-rollback check (lines 222-234) that parses the embedded sequence of BOTH the incoming and the previously-stored signed records and rejects (409) on regression. That is embedded-vs-embedded, not embedded-vs-DTO, so it does not implement the proposed fix. The resolve-path parser remains resolve-only and warn-only: parseCachedRecord treats the DB columns as authoritative and only logs a warning when the signed-record CID disagrees with latestCid (lines 553-561). So a client (holding the key) can still submit a validly-signed record whose embedded CID/sequence differ from the DTO's metadataCid/expectedSequenceNumber, and the server will accept the DTO values without a 400.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "apps/api/src/ipns/ipns.service.ts",
          "lines": "56-61",
          "observation": "Publish base64-decodes dto.record into recordBytes with only a try/catch for invalid base64; no parse of embedded fields here."
        },
        {
          "file": "apps/api/src/ipns/ipns.service.ts",
          "lines": "86-88",
          "observation": "Only validation of the record bytes on publish is verifyIpnsRecordSignature(dto.ipnsName, recordBytes); embedded CID/sequence are not extracted or compared to DTO."
        },
        {
          "file": "apps/api/src/ipns/ipns.service.ts",
          "lines": "222-234",
          "observation": "New anti-rollback: parseIpnsRecord(signedRecord) and parseIpnsRecord(existing.signedRecord), then `if (incoming.sequence < stored.sequence) throw new ConflictException` (409). Compares incoming embedded seq to STORED embedded seq, not to dto.expectedSequenceNumber/metadataCid."
        },
        {
          "file": "apps/api/src/ipns/ipns.service.ts",
          "lines": "237-248",
          "observation": "`if (existing && expectedSequenceNumber !== undefined)` compares BigInt(expectedSequenceNumber) against BigInt(existing.sequenceNumber) (the DB column), not against the embedded sequence in the signed record."
        },
        {
          "file": "apps/api/src/ipns/ipns.service.ts",
          "lines": "256-258",
          "observation": "On update, existing.latestCid = metadataCid and existing.signedRecord = Buffer.from(signedRecord) — dto.metadataCid stored verbatim; embedded record CID never compared to it."
        },
        {
          "file": "apps/api/src/ipns/ipns.service.ts",
          "lines": "298-311",
          "observation": "On create, folderIpnsRepository.create sets latestCid: metadataCid and signedRecord verbatim with sequenceNumber: '1'; no cross-check of embedded CID/sequence vs DTO."
        },
        {
          "file": "apps/api/src/ipns/ipns.service.ts",
          "lines": "553-561",
          "observation": "Resolve path parseCachedRecord: comment 'Use the DB columns as authoritative'; on `parsed.cid !== cached.latestCid` it only logger.warn(...) then returns {...parsed, cid: cached.latestCid, sequenceNumber: cached.sequenceNumber} — confirms resolve-only / warn-only."
        }
      ],
      "driftFromTodo": "Code has drifted from the todo: an anti-rollback sequence check (lines 222-234) was added that DOES call parseIpnsRecord on the signed record during the publish/upsert path — but it compares the incoming embedded sequence to the previously-stored record's embedded sequence (409 on regression), not to the DTO's expectedSequenceNumber, and it never parses/compares the embedded CID at all. Line numbers have shifted accordingly. The core publish-side embedded-vs-DTO validation that the todo describes is still absent.",
      "remediationStillValid": true,
      "notes": "The proposed fix (parse embedded CID + sequence and reject with 400 on disagreement with dto.metadataCid / dto.expectedSequenceNumber) is still valid and not yet implemented. Implementation note: parseIpnsRecord is now already imported (line 24) and invoked in upsertFolderIpns (lines 223-226), so the embedded values are readily available — the fix can reuse that parse to also (a) extract the embedded CID from incoming.value (mirroring the /ipfs/<cid> regex used at lines 508-515) and compare to dto.metadataCid, and (b) compare incoming.sequence to dto.expectedSequenceNumber when provided, throwing BadRequestException (400) on mismatch. Caveat to weigh: on first publish clients sign sequence 0 while the DB stores '1' (comment lines 296-297, 553-555), so a strict embedded-sequence-vs-expectedSequenceNumber equality check must account for the client's pre-increment convention to avoid breaking legitimate publishes."
    },
    {
      "finding": "S2",
      "status": "open",
      "summary": "Finding S2 is still fully OPEN across all four surfaces. (1) sdk-core fails closed only when all three signature fields are present and invalid, but silently skips verification (signatureVerified=false, no throw) when any field is absent. (2) The web path is strictly weaker: when a present signature is INVALID it only logger.warn's and still returns the CID with signatureVerified=false. (3) The Rust client performs no signature verification at all — its IpnsResolveResponse type does not even carry signatureV2/data/pubKey fields. (4) No resolve caller in any of the 20+ production call sites (TS or Rust) honors signatureVerified; every consumer reads only .cid / .sequenceNumber. signatureVerified is referenced outside its two producer functions only in unit tests. The proposed fix is unimplemented and still valid.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "packages/sdk-core/src/ipns/index.ts",
          "lines": "196-219",
          "observation": "Throws on invalid signature ONLY inside the `if (response.signatureV2 && response.data && response.pubKey)` branch (line 197): `if (!valid) throw new Error('IPNS signature verification failed...')` (203-205). When any field is absent it hits the else (217-219) which just `console.warn(...skipping verification)` and returns signatureVerified=false — silent downgrade still present."
        },
        {
          "file": "apps/web/src/services/ipns.service.ts",
          "lines": "177-205",
          "observation": "Web path fails OPEN: even with a present-but-INVALID signature it does `if (!valid) { logger.warn('[IPNS] Signature verification failed for', ipnsName); }` (184-185) and proceeds; key-mismatch also only logger.warn's (191); a verify exception is caught and warned (196-202); the else branch warns 'skipping verification' (204). All paths fall through to `return { cid, sequenceNumber, signatureVerified }` (207-211) — warn-and-return on invalid signature, exactly as the finding describes."
        },
        {
          "file": "crates/api-client/src/ipns.rs",
          "lines": "14-54",
          "observation": "resolve_ipns only checks HTTP status, success flag, and non-empty cid (43-51); it deserializes IpnsResolveResponse and returns it with zero signature verification. No Ed25519 / signature code anywhere in the file."
        },
        {
          "file": "crates/api-client/src/types.rs",
          "lines": "130-137",
          "observation": "IpnsResolveResponse has only `success`, `cid`, `sequence_number` — no signatureV2/data/pubKey fields, so the Rust client structurally cannot verify a signature."
        },
        {
          "file": "packages/sdk-core/src/folder/index.ts",
          "lines": "83-92",
          "observation": "Representative caller: `const resolved = await resolveIpnsRecord(...)`; uses only `resolved.cid` and `resolved.sequenceNumber` and ignores `resolved.signatureVerified`. Same pattern across cas.ts:102, file/index.ts:198 & 281, vault/index.ts:68, sdk/bin/index.ts:72 & 159, and all apps/web callers."
        },
        {
          "file": "(repo-wide grep)",
          "lines": "n/a",
          "observation": "grep for `signatureVerified` returns matches only inside the two resolveIpnsRecord definitions (sdk-core ipns/index.ts, web ipns.service.ts) and in *.test.ts assertions/mocks (bin.test.ts, file.test.ts, ipns.test.ts, vault.test.ts). No production consumer reads the flag."
        }
      ],
      "driftFromTodo": "Minor line drift only. sdk-core verification logic now lives at ipns/index.ts:182-239 (with the throw at 203-205 and silent-skip else at 217-219). The web warn-and-return logic is at ipns.service.ts:163-220 (invalid-sig warn at 184-185), and now wraps verification in its own try/catch (178-202) that also downgrades to warn. The Rust resolver is at api-client/src/ipns.rs:14-54. Behavior is unchanged from the finding; no remediation has landed.",
      "remediationStillValid": true,
      "notes": "All four proposed remediation items remain unaddressed: (a) web path still warns instead of rejecting on invalid signature; (b) no caller honors signatureVerified; (c) Rust client has no verification and its response type lacks the signature fields; (d) missing-field case is still treated as a silent skip rather than an explicit policy decision. Practical exploitability is bounded by the server being the trust source (the web comment at 174-175 notes 'the CID comes from the server's DB cache and is already trusted'), which is why this is rated Medium rather than High — but the downgrade-able verification logic is exactly as reported."
    },
    {
      "finding": "S3",
      "status": "open",
      "summary": "Finding S3 is still OPEN. Every inconsistency it describes persists in the current code. The named contradiction is verbatim: sdk-core updateFileMetadata zeroizes its caller-passed fileIpnsPrivateKey in a finally block (file/index.ts:369-373) while its sibling updateFolderMetadataAndPublish takes caller-passed ipnsPrivateKey AND folderKey but has no finally and no .fill(0) anywhere (folder/index.ts:177-242). ipns/index.ts never zeroizes any caller-passed private key (createAndPublishIpnsRecord, batchPublishIpnsRecords). vault/index.ts never zeroizes the locally-derived vaultKeyKeypair.privateKey nor the unwrapped rootFolderKey (publishVaultKeyBlob/loadVaultKeyBlob). On the Rust side, ecies.rs unwrap_key returns a raw Vec<u8> of decrypted key bytes with no Zeroizing wrapper (caller-dependent cleanup); fuse/lib.rs is internally inconsistent: struct fields use Zeroizing (lib.rs:897-899) but get_folder_key leaks raw Vec<u8> copies via .to_vec() (lib.rs:933-938), resolve_folder_key returns and BFS-queues raw Vec<u8> unwrapped folder keys (lib.rs:1595-1661), and spawn_file_meta_reencrypt mixes Zeroizing for the ipns key with raw Vec<u8> for source/dest folder keys in one signature (lib.rs:745-747). There is no documented, unified caller-owns-key convention; only scattered per-function comments tagged T-47-01 in the higher-level sdk package.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "file": "packages/sdk-core/src/file/index.ts",
          "lines": "369-373",
          "observation": "updateFileMetadata finally block zeroizes the caller-passed key: params.fileIpnsPrivateKey.fill(0); with comment 'publishWithCas never zeroes keys; the caller (this function) owns zeroing.'"
        },
        {
          "file": "packages/sdk-core/src/folder/index.ts",
          "lines": "177-242",
          "observation": "updateFolderMetadataAndPublish receives params.folderKey and params.ipnsPrivateKey (lines 180-181), passes them into publishWithCas, and returns with NO finally block and NO .fill(0) — neither caller-passed key is zeroized. Direct contradiction with updateFileMetadata."
        },
        {
          "file": "packages/sdk-core/src/folder/index.ts",
          "lines": "162-166",
          "observation": "createSubfolder only zeroizes (ipnsKeypair.privateKey.fill(0); folderKey.fill(0)) in the catch (error) branch — on success it hands keys to the caller without wiping, an implicit caller-owns convention but undocumented and applied unevenly."
        },
        {
          "file": "packages/sdk-core/src/ipns/index.ts",
          "lines": "39-98",
          "observation": "createAndPublishIpnsRecord uses params.ipnsPrivateKey in createIpnsRecord and never zeroizes it; no finally/.fill(0) anywhere in the file (batchPublishIpnsRecords at 110 likewise)."
        },
        {
          "file": "packages/sdk-core/src/vault/index.ts",
          "lines": "32-80",
          "observation": "publishVaultKeyBlob derives vaultKeyKeypair (locally owned) and never wipes vaultKeyKeypair.privateKey; loadVaultKeyBlob unwraps rootFolderKey (line 78) and returns it without any zeroization of the derived keypair private key — no .fill(0) in the file."
        },
        {
          "file": "crates/crypto/src/ecies.rs",
          "lines": "35-47",
          "observation": "unwrap_key returns Result<Vec<u8>, String> via ecies::decrypt(private_key, wrapped) — raw decrypted key bytes, no Zeroizing wrapper anywhere in the file; cleanup is entirely caller-dependent."
        },
        {
          "file": "crates/fuse/src/lib.rs",
          "lines": "1595-1661",
          "observation": "resolve_folder_key returns Result<Vec<u8>, String>; queue holds (String, Vec<u8>) unwrapped folder keys (line 1612), unwrap_key result returned raw (line 1648) — raw key Vecs dropped without zeroization."
        },
        {
          "file": "crates/fuse/src/lib.rs",
          "lines": "933-938",
          "observation": "get_folder_key returns Option<Vec<u8>> via self.root_folder_key.to_vec() / folder_key.to_vec(), leaking unzeroized copies of Zeroizing-protected struct fields (fields declared Zeroizing at lines 897-899)."
        },
        {
          "file": "crates/fuse/src/lib.rs",
          "lines": "745-747",
          "observation": "spawn_file_meta_reencrypt signature mixes file_ipns_private_key: Zeroizing<Vec<u8>> with source_folder_key: Vec<u8> and dest_folder_key: Vec<u8> — inconsistent key cleanup within one function. spawn_metadata_publish (lines 416-417) takes folder_key/ipns_private_key as raw Vec<u8>."
        }
      ],
      "driftFromTodo": "Moderate drift, all cosmetic/locational — the substance is unchanged. (1) Line numbers shifted: updateFileMetadata zeroization is now file/index.ts:369-373 (finding cited Phase-44-era code); updateFolderMetadataAndPublish is folder/index.ts:177-242. (2) The Rust reencrypt function the finding alludes to is now named spawn_file_meta_reencrypt (lib.rs:741), distinct from spawn_metadata_publish (lib.rs:412). (3) Partial progress exists in the higher-level sdk package: client.ts and shared-write.ts now carry per-function 'caller owns zeroing' comments tagged T-47-01, with tests asserting non-zeroization of caller-owned vaultPrivateKey — but this is per-function documentation in sdk, not the unified, documented convention across sdk-core that the todo proposes, and the sdk-core contradiction is untouched.",
      "remediationStillValid": true,
      "notes": "No central caller-owns-key convention is documented anywhere in sdk-core or docs/ (grep for 'caller-owns-key', 'buffer-owning boundary', 'key ownership convention' returns nothing in source). The only convention text is scattered per-function comments in the sdk package (client.ts:1436/1517/2405, shared-write.ts:520/527/579) and one sdk-core comment (file/index.ts:371). The proposed fix remains the correct remediation: (a) document and apply a uniform caller-owns/zeroize-at-buffer-owner convention across sdk-core, (b) reconcile updateFolderMetadataAndPublish to match updateFileMetadata (or vice-versa, with explicit caller-owns documentation), and (c) audit the Rust raw-Vec<u8> key returns (ecies::unwrap_key consumers, resolve_folder_key, get_folder_key, spawn_metadata_publish / spawn_file_meta_reencrypt folder-key params) to use Zeroizing consistently. Severity remains Medium: these are defense-in-depth memory-hygiene gaps, not a direct key-disclosure vulnerability."
    }
  ]
}
